# README maintenance

| File | Audience |
|------|----------|
| **`README.md`** | Short, fun, user-facing (no file paths or deploy plumbing) |
| **`docs/DEVELOPMENT.md`** | Dev hub: architecture, local setup, env, links to other docs |
| **`docs/FEATURES.md`** | Detailed product / route / notification / worker behavior |
| **`docs/DEPLOYMENT.md`** | Tests, CI, Heroku dynos, build pipeline |
| **`docs/OPERATIONS.md`** | **Critical operations** — cache, SW, pick timing, troubleshooting |

## When to update

In the **same change** (or immediately after):

| Change | Update |
|--------|--------|
| Player-visible feature, league, tab, rule | `README.md` + `docs/FEATURES.md` |
| Local dev, architecture, env | `docs/DEVELOPMENT.md` |
| CI, Heroku, `heroku-postbuild`, tests gate | `docs/DEPLOYMENT.md` |
| PWA/cache/SW, workers, notification logic, pick/scoring internals users depend on | `docs/OPERATIONS.md` (+ `docs/FEATURES.md` if user-visible) |

One line in `README.md` is enough when users only notice the effect (e.g. “app updates after deploy” → point to ops in dev docs, not full detail in README).

Do **not** rewrite docs for copy/CSS-only tweaks or refactors with no behavior change.

## Agent checklist

1. User-visible? **`README.md`** — brief.
2. Which technical doc? Use the table above; prefer editing an existing subsection.
3. Critical production behavior always lands in **`docs/OPERATIONS.md`**.

